/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  cstr04_adiabatic  --  an ADIABATIC continuous stirred-tank reactor.

  The outlet temperature is an UNKNOWN, solved WITH the reaction extents:

      xi_j = r_j(xi, T) V_R          (R design equations)
      H_out(T, xi) = H_in            (one enthalpy balance)

  There is NO dH_rxn source term.  With the enthalpy on the ELEMENTS datum, the
  heat of reaction already lives inside H: as reactants become products the
  formation enthalpies differ and T rises on its own.  Q_kW = 0 by construction.

  Since the acetic-acid correction (docs/design/acetic-acid-latent-heat-closure.md)
  this LIQUID esterification is nearly thermoneutral (dH_rxn about -2.7 kJ/mol
  at 298 K, NBS-consistent; the engine used to print about -25.7 kJ/mol), so the
  adiabatic rise is a few K (T_out 355.3 K from the 350 K feed) and the energy
  balance has ONE steady state: the scan finds one.  A non-isothermal CSTR CAN
  have up to three steady states, but that needs a strongly exothermic reaction;
  this one does not.  `T_guess` is kept (it is the declared aid that selects the
  branch where there are several) and here it cannot matter.

  The original teaching point of this case (an adiabatic reactor that heats
  itself by ~100 K) needs a genuinely exothermic reaction; a replacement case on
  such a reaction is planned.  No parameter was changed to recover the old rise.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        reactor;
        type        cstr;
        in          feed;
        outputs     ( reactorOut );
        operation
        {
            V_R          0.005;         // m^3
            thermalMode  adiabatic;     // <-- T_out is a RESULT
            T_guess      350;           // K  -- which branch the Newton starts from
        }
        reactions   ( esterification_etac );   // one-element list -> the general path
    }
);
